{"subjectId":"python","id":"functools-module","title":"functools Deep Dive","group":"Functions","order":28,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>functools</code> is the standard library's toolbox for working <em>with functions</em>: caching, pre-filling arguments, building decorators, generic dispatch and ordering.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>At a glance</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>partial(f, *a, **kw)</code></td><td>New callable with some arguments pre-filled</td></tr><tr><td><code>partialmethod</code></td><td><code>partial</code> for methods defined in a class body</td></tr><tr><td><code>reduce(f, it, init)</code></td><td>Fold an iterable into a single value</td></tr><tr><td><code>cache</code> / <code>lru_cache(maxsize)</code></td><td>Memoize results by arguments</td></tr><tr><td><code>cached_property</code></td><td>Compute an attribute once per instance, then store it</td></tr><tr><td><code>wraps</code> / <code>update_wrapper</code></td><td>Copy name, docstring, etc. onto a wrapper</td></tr><tr><td><code>total_ordering</code></td><td>Fill in <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code> from <code>__eq__</code> + one comparison</td></tr><tr><td><code>singledispatch</code></td><td>Choose an implementation by the <strong>type</strong> of the first argument</td></tr><tr><td><code>cmp_to_key</code></td><td>Turn an old-style comparator into a <code>key=</code> function</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-puzzle\"></i>partial &amp; partialmethod</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from functools import partial, partialmethod\n\ndef power(base, exp):\n    return base ** exp\n\nsquare = partial(power, exp=2)\ncube = partial(power, exp=3)\nprint(square(7), cube(2))                    # 49 8\nprint(square.func.__name__, square.keywords) # power {'exp': 2}\n\nparse_hex = partial(int, base=16)\nprint(list(map(parse_hex, [\"ff\", \"10\"])))    # [255, 16]\n\nclass Light:\n    def set(self, state):\n        self.state = state\n        return state\n    on = partialmethod(set, \"on\")\n    off = partialmethod(set, \"off\")\n\nprint(Light().on())                          # on</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>partial</code> beats a lambda when you want an inspectable, picklable callable (e.g. passing work to a process pool) — lambdas can't be pickled.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-minimize\"></i>reduce</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from functools import reduce\nimport operator\n\nprint(reduce(operator.add, [1, 2, 3, 4]))           # 10\nprint(reduce(operator.mul, range(1, 6), 1))         # 120\nprint(reduce(lambda acc, d: {**acc, **d}, [{\"a\": 1}, {\"b\": 2}, {\"a\": 3}], {}))   # {'a': 3, 'b': 2}\nprint(reduce(max, [3, 9, 2]))                       # 9</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>reduce</code> on an empty iterable without an initial value raises <code>TypeError</code>. Prefer <code>sum</code>, <code>math.prod</code>, <code>max</code>, <code>any</code>/<code>all</code> or a loop when they express the intent better.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>cache &amp; lru_cache</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from functools import lru_cache, cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=2)\ndef square(x):\n    return x * x\n\nsquare(1); square(2); square(1)      # hit for 1\nsquare(3)                            # evicts 2 (least recently used)\nsquare(2)                            # miss again\ninfo = square.cache_info()\nprint(info.hits, info.misses, info.currsize)   # 1 4 2\nsquare.cache_clear()\nprint(square.cache_info().currsize)            # 0\n\n@lru_cache(typed=True)\ndef kind(x):\n    return type(x).__name__\nprint(kind(1), kind(1.0))            # int float   ← typed: 1 and 1.0 cached separately\n\n@cache\ndef total(xs):\n    return sum(xs)\ntry:\n    total([1, 2])                    # arguments are dict keys → must be hashable\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(e)                         # unhashable type: 'list'\nprint(total((1, 2)))                 # 3</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>@cache</code></th><th><code>@lru_cache(maxsize=128)</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Size</td><td>Unbounded</td><td>Bounded; evicts least-recently-used</td></tr><tr><td>Speed</td><td>Slightly faster (no bookkeeping)</td><td>Small LRU overhead</td></tr><tr><td>Use for</td><td>Recursion / DP with a bounded input space</td><td>Long-running services with unbounded inputs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Method caching pitfall</div><p>Decorating a <strong>method</strong> with <code>@cache</code> caches on <code>self</code> too: every instance stays alive as long as the cache does (a memory leak), and instances must be hashable. Prefer <code>cached_property</code>, a module-level cached function, or a per-instance dict.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i>cached_property</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from functools import cached_property\n\nclass Report:\n    def __init__(self, rows):\n        self.rows = rows\n        self.computed = 0\n\n    @cached_property\n    def total(self):\n        self.computed += 1           # expensive work, done once\n        return sum(self.rows)\n\nr = Report([5, 10, 20])\nprint(r.total, r.total, r.computed)  # 35 35 1\ndel r.total                          # invalidate: the next access recomputes\nprint(r.total, r.computed)           # 35 2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>cached_property</code> stores the value in the instance <code>__dict__</code> under the same name, so later lookups never call the function. It needs a <code>__dict__</code> (not compatible with <code>__slots__</code> alone). Unlike <code>property</code>, it is writable.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-gift\"></i>wraps &amp; update_wrapper</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from functools import wraps, update_wrapper\n\ndef plain(f):\n    def wrapper(*a, **kw):\n        return f(*a, **kw)\n    return wrapper\n\ndef proper(f):\n    @wraps(f)                         # = update_wrapper(wrapper, f)\n    def wrapper(*a, **kw):\n        return f(*a, **kw)\n    return wrapper\n\n@plain\ndef area(r):\n    \"\"\"Circle area.\"\"\"\n@proper\ndef volume(r):\n    \"\"\"Sphere volume.\"\"\"\n\nprint(area.__name__, area.__doc__)        # wrapper None\nprint(volume.__name__, volume.__doc__)    # volume Sphere volume.\nprint(volume.__wrapped__.__name__)        # volume   ← the original, for introspection</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i>total_ordering</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from functools import total_ordering\n\n@total_ordering\nclass Grade:\n    order = \"FDCBA\"\n    def __init__(self, letter):\n        self.letter = letter\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        return self.letter == other.letter\n    def __lt__(self, other):\n        return self.order.index(self.letter) &lt; self.order.index(other.letter)\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return self.letter\n\nprint(Grade(\"B\") &gt; Grade(\"C\"), Grade(\"A\") &lt;= Grade(\"A\"))   # True True\nprint(sorted([Grade(\"C\"), Grade(\"A\"), Grade(\"F\")]))        # [F, C, A]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-fork\"></i>singledispatch — overloading by type</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from functools import singledispatch, singledispatchmethod\n\n@singledispatch\ndef to_json(value):\n    raise TypeError(f\"can't serialise {type(value).__name__}\")\n\n@to_json.register\ndef _(value: int):\n    return str(value)\n\n@to_json.register\ndef _(value: str):\n    return f'\"{value}\"'\n\n@to_json.register(list)\ndef _(value):\n    return \"[\" + \", \".join(to_json(v) for v in value) + \"]\"\n\nprint(to_json([1, \"a\", [2]]))       # [1, \"a\", [2]]\nprint(to_json(True))                # True   ← bool is a subclass of int\ntry:\n    to_json(1.5)\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(e)                        # can't serialise float\n\nclass Area:\n    @singledispatchmethod\n    def of(self, shape):\n        raise NotImplementedError\n    @of.register\n    def _(self, side: int):\n        return side * side\n    @of.register\n    def _(self, dims: tuple):\n        return dims[0] * dims[1]\n\nprint(Area().of(3), Area().of((2, 5)))   # 9 10</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Dispatch looks only at the <strong>first</strong> argument's type and follows the MRO, so a handler for <code>int</code> also handles <code>bool</code>. It's the standard-library answer to “function overloading”.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-settings\"></i>cmp_to_key &amp; the operator module</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from functools import cmp_to_key\nfrom operator import itemgetter, attrgetter, methodcaller\n\ndef by_length_then_reverse_alpha(a, b):\n    if len(a) != len(b):\n        return len(a) - len(b)\n    return (a &lt; b) - (a &gt; b)          # reverse alphabetical\n\nwords = [\"bb\", \"a\", \"cc\", \"c\"]\nprint(sorted(words, key=cmp_to_key(by_length_then_reverse_alpha)))   # ['c', 'a', 'cc', 'bb']\n\nprint(itemgetter(0, 2)(\"xyz\"))                     # ('x', 'z')\nprint(attrgetter(\"real\")(3 + 4j))                  # 3.0\nprint(list(map(methodcaller(\"upper\"), [\"a\", \"b\"])))   # ['A', 'B']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-plus\"></i><code>operator.add</code>, <code>mul</code>, …</h3><p>Function versions of operators — pass to <code>reduce</code>, <code>map</code>, <code>accumulate</code>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-details\"></i><code>itemgetter(k)</code></h3><p><code>lambda x: x[k]</code> — faster, and accepts several keys.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-dots\"></i><code>attrgetter('a.b')</code></h3><p><code>lambda x: x.a.b</code> — dotted paths allowed.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-phone-call\"></i><code>methodcaller('m', arg)</code></h3><p><code>lambda x: x.m(arg)</code>.</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How does lru_cache work?</summary><div><p>It stores results in a dict keyed by the call's arguments (so they must be hashable) and keeps a doubly linked list of recency. On a hit it returns the stored value and marks it recent; when the cache exceeds <code>maxsize</code>, it evicts the least recently used entry. <code>cache_info()</code> reports hits and misses.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>partial vs lambda?</summary><div><p>Both pre-fill arguments. <code>partial</code> is introspectable (<code>.func</code>, <code>.args</code>, <code>.keywords</code>), picklable when the wrapped function is, and binds values immediately — no late-binding surprise in loops. A lambda is more flexible (can reorder or transform arguments).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you get function overloading in Python?</summary><div><p>Python has no signature-based overloading — a later <code>def</code> replaces the earlier one. Use default/keyword arguments, or <code>functools.singledispatch</code> to dispatch on the first argument's type (<code>singledispatchmethod</code> for methods).</p></div></details></section>"}